    "q-257193": {
      "dom_id": "step-q257193",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n$1.014$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(0) & 6(2)-4(0) \\\\ 6(2)-4(0) & 6-4(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6(2)(0)-2(0)^{2}-1 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}-4(2)(0)+6(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-2)-12\\cdot 12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}-142 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.014 \\\\ 0.083\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
